WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.020
So last night we had the third Democratic
presidential debate.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.820
This one with 10 candidates total instead
of 10

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:11.170
and that it's across two nights happened on
just one night and this was actually in just

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:16.919
about every sense, the best debate so far
and I'll tell you who I think did well.

00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:23.140
I'll tell you what I think the impact of it
will be, but as far as the debate goes, yes,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.499
the ABC, Univision moderators still asked
cartoonishly stupid questions, which I will

00:00:28.499 --> 00:00:33.070
discuss, but I had sort of complained on Twitter
three hours.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:39.710
Do we really need three hours of debate, particularly
when it, most of these 25 minutes go by before

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:45.430
the first question is you and ask between
sports like introductions and an immediate

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.800
commercial break and a video montage and opening
statements.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.910
You don't get to the first question very often
until nearly 30 minutes in.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:53.910
They didn't do that.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.530
They started with the first question very,
very quickly or at least opening statements.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.210
It ended actually like 20 minutes shy of the
three hour mark and in the end all the other

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.900
debates had gone long.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.409
This one went short and was far more substantive.

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:13.060
They spent, I think it was the first, uh,
30 to 35 minutes of questions I'm going from

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.259
memory here was just healthcare.

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:20.869
Did we learn an insane amount about everybody's
health care plans?

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:21.869
Maybe not.

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:26.630
But what did happen, and this is the whole
point, when you spend more time on one topic,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.389
even when it is 10 people on the stage, which
is a whole other problem, even if you don't

00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:36.899
get that much more detail from most people
about their plans, it starts to become clear

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:41.930
who has nothing more than just two or three
bullet points about what they would do.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.710
Joe Biden starting to stumble about the dynamics
of if you can't afford it or if you can't

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.469
afford it or whatever else the case may be.

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:53.380
Elizabeth Warren, who had been accused of
backing off of Medicare for all, at least

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.279
in rhetoric during yesterday's debate, coming
back fully and saying Medicare for all is

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:04.700
the way, uh, various doodly rebutting this
idea that taxes would go up by saying very

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.700
clearly.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.700
Yeah.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.130
But who cares if taxes go up, the total cost
goes down because you don't have premiums

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.240
and copays and all of this other stuff.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:16.240
Um, let's talk about some of the different,
uh, elements of this that I think will impact

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.430
it overall.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.300
Let me get out of the way.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:25.540
The moderators did a better job than in many
previous debates and by focusing longer periods

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:33.400
of time on one issue, they were playing a
role of furthering depth on some topics including

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.470
climate change and health care, et cetera.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.100
But there was still no shortage of completely
ridiculous questions.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:43.220
At one point, asking Bernie Sanders to say,
uh, hey kid, you're, you're a socialist and

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.110
Nicolas Maduro in Venezuela is a socialist.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.860
Uh, what do you make of that?

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.920
And then Bernie Sanders having to explain
different types of socialism.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.870
This actually, I set it on Twitter and some
people agreed with me, some people went after

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:55.970
me.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:56.970
Bernie's policy.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:01.820
If Bernie is a democratic socialist privately,
okay, maybe he is Bernie's policies or social

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:08.940
democratic policies in line with capitalist
countries in northern Europe that are well-regulated

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:15.790
and use the fruits of the capitalist system
to disproportionately help the least among

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:16.790
us.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.640
That's Bernie's policy and this is one of
those things where I've been saying it for

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.990
years at this point and like 75% of my audience
is with me, 25 is not.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:29.970
Um, if he all along just said, uh, I considered
myself a democratic socialist in my youth

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.550
or whatever and not even mentioned that I'm
a social democrat.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:38.170
My policies are in line with countries that
are capitalist countries, Nicolas Maduro and

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.810
that form of Pho populist, so-called socialism,
whatever.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.350
It has nothing to do with what I'm doing.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.400
And to even suggest the comparison is absolutely
absurd.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.350
He can't do that because he does call himself
a socialist and then he has to make finer

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:56.330
distinctions, whatever the question was ridiculous,
didn't further our understanding at all of

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.500
a Bernie Sanders platform.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.250
They asked Corey Booker about is he gonna
push his Vegan Diet on everyone?

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:07.260
I'm paraphrasing here just ridiculously stupid
question, but potentially a missed opportunity

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.690
for Cory booker where he could've talked about
animal welfare.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.450
He could have talked about the importance
of diet.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.570
I mean he could've been humanizing in self
humanizing in some way and it ended up just

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.820
being a stupid question and kind of a non-answer.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:26.120
Now one of the bigger narratives that I was
paying attention to, which is a real narrative,

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:29.160
unlike corporate media, often foments totally
bogus narratives.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.160
Nobody cares about.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.840
I think it was before the second debate.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.720
CNN was pushing what will happen when booted,
judge and O'Rourke go against each other.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:37.720
It's like nothing.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:38.720
No one cares about that.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.430
They're each polling 2% totally fabricated
narrative.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.940
There was a real question yesterday.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.060
They were really two primary questions for
me.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:50.800
Number one, how to what degree would Bernie
and Warren who are the two progressive front

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.560
runners, Biden, of course, being more of a
center left front runner.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.190
Would Bernie and Warren start to go against
each other to any degree to try to distinguish

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:04.300
themselves from each other, often just being
lumped together as the two progressive's.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.389
The other question was would Bernie and Warren
either together or separately start to go

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:12.139
after Joe Biden now that he is sort of within
striking range?

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:18.500
And the answer was Bernie went against Biden
way harder than Warren and I, I may just not

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.950
be remembering, but I don't really remember
Warren going after Biden at all, may be one

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:25.740
sort of lightly.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.180
But Bernie went after Biden on the Iraq war
vote.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:35.040
Bernie went after Biden on healthcare, and
some of those moments were arguably Bernie's

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.120
best moments.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.120
Now.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.120
What did not go so well for Bernie?

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.520
He unfortunately either had some kind of bronchitis
or was just horse from so many events, but

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.070
he had like a half lost voice.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:49.480
He didn't lose his voice, but particularly
the first half of the debate, his voice was

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.910
not strong.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:55.220
And by sheer you could say it's chance or
if you're more conspiratorial, you could say

00:05:55.220 --> 00:06:00.060
it's because ABC wanted to help Elizabeth
Warren very often.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:06.970
Both Bernie and Warren would rebut what Joe
Biden would say, but it would almost always

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:09.710
be Elizabeth Warren who got the first opportunity.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.770
And as I've said before, even though they
come from different political backgrounds,

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:17.910
Bernie and Warren end up very similar on policy
and thus their rebuttals to Joe Biden were

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:22.890
very, very similar and merely by virtue of
Elizabeth Warren getting the first rebuttal

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:28.090
against Biden and Bernie Sanders getting the
second rebuttal, which often contained a 90%

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.889
the same substance and Bernie had half lost
his voice.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:36.669
I think the optics were much stronger for
Elizabeth Warren in terms of giving her perspective

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:42.540
even though as I said, she didn't really attack
Biden in the way that, uh, some thought maybe

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:43.710
she would be doing.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:47.850
Uh, one thing that became very clear, and
again this is uh, this is why we want more

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.320
depth and I would want way more with fewer
candidates on stage.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:57.520
When you spend 40 minutes on one topic and
uh, you actually are put in a position where

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:03.750
you have to go seventh and explain what is
your policy one dead giveaway that you don't

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:08.420
really have a policy that's unique in any
way is when the candidate would be asked,

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:12.260
like the sixth person would say, okay, what
is your position on what should happen with

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.550
trade?

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:19.169
And they just talk about how Trump is doing
things that are bad and restate very general

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:20.590
principles on trade.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:24.800
This became very, very transparent, uh, with
a number of different candidates.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.540
There was no, you know, Camila Harris on trade
had nothing unique or irrelevant to say she

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:30.610
criticized Trump.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:33.449
It's like you're running against each other
to go against Trump.

00:07:33.449 --> 00:07:34.990
You're, you all disagree with Trump.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.900
I would limit the amount that you're attacking
Trump at this point in the third debate and

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.350
had nothing particularly interesting to talk
about.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:46.450
Uh, with regard to trade, there were a lot
of examples of this, a strong moment for Beto

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:52.520
or Rourke was when he was asked, are you going
to just confiscate AK 47 and ar fifteens or

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:53.600
what are you going to do?

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.460
And it was sort of a right wing framing of
the gun question.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.520
But I respect Beto for saying yes we are,
we are going to confiscate them.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.010
Other countries have done it.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.390
What's going on here is a disaster.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.500
We are going to confiscate some of those really
deadly weapons.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.890
The pro gun crowd is going to hate it.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:08.890
Either way.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.230
The pro gun crowd is going to say that the
entire debate was filled with people that

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.720
want to grab guns even if they don't say it,
even if you don't agree with the policy.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.550
I actually respect Beto for saying it.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:23.550
Uh, Andrew Yang had his best debate by far
and I got a lot of emails from Yang Gang people

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:24.910
who felt that he didn't do so well.

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:30.360
I think he actually did, did uh, his best
where he suffered his, he's almost too polite

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.200
and he will stop short of his full time rather
than going over it.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.159
And this was one of the reasons why his speaking
time was low.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:39.570
Also, they just don't go to him nearly as
often.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:45.160
He made a joke at the beginning of when talking
about healthcare, about I'm Asian, so I know

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.020
a lot of doctors or something like that.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.390
And I actually do find it funny when he says
stuff like that when he says, I'm the opposite

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.390
of Trump.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.380
I'm an Asian guy who likes math or whatever.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:59.220
I don't know if it actually endears him a
two people or if it's just kind of a thing.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.300
You hear a candidate say, we're, we're, it
feels like he's there trying to get attention.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.360
I'm sort of blind to it because I'm so sucked
into this stuff.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.530
So I'd be curious what you think about that.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:15.240
On speaking time, Biden and Warren got the
most followed by booker then Bernie Kama Harris

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.390
Mayor Pete Castro club.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:21.279
Machar Beto and Yang would the least speaking
time.

00:09:21.279 --> 00:09:22.279
Not surprisingly.

00:09:22.279 --> 00:09:24.541
Overall, I believe Elizabeth, I don't know
who won it.

00:09:24.541 --> 00:09:26.420
It was less clear in this case.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.810
I think Elizabeth Warren did really well.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:35.350
I think, uh, Andrew Yang had his best debates
so far, who Leon Castro was very strong on

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:38.740
a number of issues including immigration and
criminal justice reform.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:43.649
Senator Bernie Sanders was strong, said the
right things, but again, was diminished with

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:45.100
the horse voice.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:49.440
And by virtue of having to go second after
Warren in a lot of the rebuttals.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.390
But those are the four that I think did the
best.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.700
Some people on Twitter yesterday were saying
Kamala Harris crushed it.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:55.700
I don't know.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:56.700
I don't know what debate you watched.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.630
I didn't see Kamala Harris crush anything.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:04.170
But in the end, I don't expect big polling
moves other than maybe within the top three.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:11.029
And the reason why is Warren Biden and Bernie
have substantive records on policy beyond

00:10:11.029 --> 00:10:13.100
everybody else on stage.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.890
They have preexisting basis of support.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:22.010
They have a sort of different visions generally
about how they would govern.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:27.160
And they have constituencies that just don't
really allow that much room for other candidates.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:31.980
So quite frankly, there's not that much support
for the other candidates to gain because the

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:35.330
top three now have solid basis of support.

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:40.250
Uh, we will see how the polls change between
now and the fourth debate, which will be again,

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.070
a two night thing with 11 candidates October
15th and 16th.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.460
I think it is a mistake to be adding more
candidates.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:48.709
We should be calling them even more.

00:10:48.709 --> 00:10:50.950
But alas, that is not the case.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:54.890
Warren Castro, Yang Bernie, not in any particular
order.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.779
The winners of last night's debate

00:10:56.779 --> 00:10:57.779
agree with me.

00:10:57.779 --> 00:10:58.779
Disagree.

00:10:58.779 --> 00:11:35.200
Let me know.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:48.360
I'm

00:11:48.360 --> 00:12:02.170
on

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:02.649
twitter@impactthedavidpakmanshowatdavidpakman.com

